\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Convex roof of the linear entropy $S_L=1-\tr \rho _A^2$ for well-known bound entangled (PPT) and NPT states. Bars: lower bounds from the simplified hierarchy at the levels $(k,m)$ shown in the legend (blue, light to dark, up to $k=m=3$; green: $(2,4)$, for comparison with $(3,3)$). Markers: upper bound from the decomposition extracted from the SDP solution at $k=m=3$ (orange diamonds), upper bound from HJW gradient descent over decompositions (black crosses), drawn left and right of each group; values above the axis are clipped and printed. Every value is divided by the reference printed above each group: the exact roof for two qubits (marked $^*$), the best upper bound otherwise. The level $(2,4)$ is missing for Horodecki $3\times 3$, Piani $4\times 4$, where the SDP exceeds the memory of our machine (more than 32\,GB). Members of parametric families: GenTiles2 UPB ($3\times 4$); Chessboard (extremal member); Cross-hatch (grid state); Rank-4 PPT (Nat. Commun. 5, 6297 (2014)); Horodecki $3\times 3$ ($a=0.5$); Horodecki $2\times 4$ ($b=0.5$); Werner $2\times 2$ (singlet weight $p=0.8$); Gisin ($\lambda =0.9$, $\theta =\pi /8$); MEMS ($C=0.8$); Bell + $\ket {01}$ ($p=0.6$).}}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:namedLinearEntropy}{{2}{2}{}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Relative gap between the upper bounds on $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle S_L$}\mathaccent "0362{S_L}$ and the SDP lower bound at $k=m=3$, for the states of Fig.~\ref {fig:namedLinearEntropy} (PPT left of the dashed line, NPT right). Orange: decomposition extracted from the SDP solution (Sec.~\ref {sec:extractingTheDecomposition}); black: HJW gradient descent from random starts. Points at $10^{-10}$ are gaps below solver precision.}}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:upperBounds}{{3}{3}{}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Polynomials $P\leq F$ selected by the LP for four two-qubit states (Werner $2\times 2$: singlet weight $p=0.8$; Gisin: $\lambda =0.9$, $\theta =\pi /8$; MEMS: $C=0.8$; Bell + $\ket {01}$: $p=0.6$), as functions of the Schmidt coefficient $\alpha _1$; the spectrum is $(\alpha _1,1-\alpha _1)$, so both $F$ and $P$ are symmetric under $\alpha _1\to 1-\alpha _1$. (a) Entanglement entropy at level $(k,m)=(3,4)$. (b) Vidal monotone $E_1=\min (\alpha _1,1-\alpha _1)$ at level $(3,3)$. Dots: the Schmidt coefficients $(1\pm \sqrt  {1-C^2})/2$ shared by all states of the optimal (Wootters) decomposition, where $P$ touches $F$ when the bound is tight.}}{4}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:polynomials}{{4}{4}{}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Lower bounds on (a) the entanglement of formation and (b) the convex roof of the Vidal monotone $E_1$ from the SDP with embedded LP (polynomials of degree $m$), for the states of Fig.~\ref {fig:namedLinearEntropy}. Bars: lower bounds from the simplified hierarchy at the levels $(k,m)$ shown in the legend (blue, light to dark, up to $k=m=3$; green: $(2,4)$, for comparison with $(3,3)$). Markers: upper bound from the decomposition extracted from the SDP solution at $k=m=3$ (orange diamonds), upper bound from HJW gradient descent over decompositions (black crosses), drawn left and right of each group; values above the axis are clipped and printed. Every value is divided by the reference printed above each group: the exact roof for two qubits (marked $^*$), the best upper bound otherwise. The level $(2,4)$ is missing for Horodecki $3\times 3$, Piani $4\times 4$, where the SDP exceeds the memory of our machine (more than 32\,GB). The decomposition giving the orange markers is extracted from the SDP of the optimal polynomial $P^*\leq F$ found at $k=m=3$. For two qubits, odd $m$ gives the same polynomials as $m-1$.}}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:namedNonpolynomial}{{5}{5}{}{figure.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces LOCC convertibility from the pure state $\ket {\psi }$ with entanglement spectrum $(0.6,0.3,0.1)$ to the two-qutrit states $\rho (p,\theta )=p\dyad {\Phi _3}+(1-p)\dyad {\chi _\theta }$, with $\ket {\Phi _3}$ maximally entangled and $\ket {\chi _\theta }=\cos \theta \ket {00}+\sin \theta \ket {11}$, from the SDP of Eq.~\eqref  {eq:SDPlocc} at level $(k,m)=(3,4)$. Blue: the decomposition extracted from the optimal $\Phi _{k,m}$ satisfies Eq.~\eqref  {eq:JPcriterion}, so $\psi \to \rho $ [Theorem~\ref {thm:LOCC}(ii)]. Orange: $s_{k,m}$ exceeds the grid correction, so $\psi \nrightarrow \rho $ [Theorem~\ref {thm:LOCC}(i)]. Gray: neither certificate applies at this level.}}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:locc}{{6}{6}{}{figure.6}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Side length of the SDP variable and solve time of the bound on the convex roof of the linear entropy, for representative states of each dimension, at the levels $(2,3)$ and $(2,4)$ of the simplified relaxation [Eq.~\eqref  {eq:marginals_SDP}] and at $k=m=3$, where it is the full SDP of Eq.~\eqref  {eq:SDPcroof1}. Raw: $D^k d_A^{m-k}$, the size of $\Phi _{k,m}$ without reductions ($D=d_Ad_B$). Reduced: $\binom  {k+r-1}{k}d_A^{m-k}$, after restricting the $k$ full copies to the symmetric subspace of the support of $\rho $, with $r=\operatorname  {rank}\rho $ (Appendix~\ref {app:SymRed}); in addition, the positivity constraints are split into Schur blocks and the marginal copies $A_{k+1},\dots  ,A_m$ are parametrized by their permutation invariance. At equal reduced size, levels with marginal copies ($m>k$) are slower, because of their additional partial-transpose constraints and permutation invariance equalities. Times: interior-point solver QICS on a 16-core laptop with 23\,GB of memory, measured with 13 runs in parallel (one thread each), so they are indicative only. OOM: the solver needs more than the 23\,GB of memory; --: not attempted.}}{7}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:dimensions}{{1}{7}{}{table.1}{}}
